{"black_box_warning":{"current_value":"Current row contains the NAYZILAM boxed warning text, but the source field cites DailyMed and is not acceptable for the FDA black-box field under the audit policy.","fda_source_urls":["https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%222b29422e-54d5-4a49-8522-e9cf752368c3%22&limit=5","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf"],"proposed_value":"No change required to fda_black_box_warning text; replace only fda_black_box_warning_source with an FDA/openFDA or FDA label source.","rationale":"FDA NAYZILAM labeling carries the boxed warning for concomitant opioid use, abuse/misuse/addiction, and dependence/withdrawal. The current source string relies on DailyMed, which the instructions prohibit for this field.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified"},"blocking_questions":[],"fact_checks":[{"current_value":"midazolam","field":"generic_name","proposed_value":"midazolam","rationale":"FDA labels and PubMed RCT records identify the active drug as midazolam.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33140403/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31140596/"]},{"current_value":"","field":"alternate_generic_names","proposed_value":"","rationale":"No true alternate generic name was identified from the checked sources. USL261 is an investigational/development code for intranasal midazolam and belongs in pubmed_search_aliases, not alternate_generic_names.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31140596/","https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT01390220"]},{"current_value":"Nayzilam; Seizalam; Versed","field":"trade_names","proposed_value":"Nayzilam; Seizalam; Versed","rationale":"Nayzilam and Seizalam are verified from FDA sources. Versed is plausible as a historical midazolam trade name, but this row bundle does not provide a trusted source directly supporting it.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=508215"]},{"current_value":"Nayzilam; midazolam nasal spray","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"Nayzilam; midazolam nasal spray; USL261","rationale":"The phase III RCT and ClinicalTrials.gov records use USL261 for the intranasal midazolam product; adding it improves search recall without creating a duplicate drug row.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31140596/","https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT01390220"]},{"current_value":"Yes","field":"available_in_us","proposed_value":"Yes","rationale":"FDA labels verify U.S.-approved midazolam products including NAYZILAM and SEIZALAM.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Available in US","field":"filter_availability","proposed_value":"Available in US","rationale":"Consistent with current FDA-labeled products.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"1985 injection; 2019 nasal seizure-cluster product","field":"year_fda_cleared","proposed_value":"1985 initial U.S. approval for midazolam injection/sedation; 2018 SEIZALAM IM injection for status epilepticus; 2019 NAYZILAM nasal spray for seizure clusters","rationale":"The current value is directionally correct but omits the FDA-approved SEIZALAM status-epilepticus product approved in 2018.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215868s000lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=508215","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Seizure clusters / rescue; Status epilepticus","field":"epilepsy_type","proposed_value":"Seizure clusters / rescue; Status epilepticus","rationale":"NAYZILAM is indicated for seizure clusters/acute repetitive seizures; SEIZALAM is indicated for adult status epilepticus.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Seizure clusters / rescue; Status epilepticus","field":"filter_epilepsy_type","proposed_value":"Seizure clusters / rescue; Status epilepticus","rationale":"Matches FDA-labeled seizure-cluster and status-epilepticus uses.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Nasal spray; buccal/oromucosal solution in some markets; IV/IM injection; oral syrup for sedation","field":"formulations_available","proposed_value":"Nasal spray; buccal/oromucosal solution in some non-US markets; IV/IM injection; oral syrup for procedural sedation","rationale":"Nasal spray and IM/IV injection are supported by FDA labels; buccal/oromucosal midazolam is supported by EMA/eMC sources. Oral syrup is plausible but needs a trusted row source if retained.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215868s000lbl.pdf","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/medicines/human/EPAR/buccolam","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/search?q=buccolam"]},{"current_value":"IV/IM injection; Liquid; Rescue formulation","field":"filter_formulation","proposed_value":"IV/IM injection; Liquid; Rescue formulation","rationale":"Supported by FDA-labeled IM status-epilepticus injection and intranasal rescue product; liquid solution formulations are shown in labels.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Rescue dosing individualized by product/age/weight","field":"minimum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"NAYZILAM: 5 mg intranasal initial dose for patients 12 years and older; SEIZALAM: 10 mg IM for adults with status epilepticus; non-US buccal/oromucosal dosing varies by age/weight and product","rationale":"FDA labels give product-specific approved dosing; the current wording is too vague for U.S. products.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/medicines/human/EPAR/buccolam"]},{"current_value":"Rescue dosing individualized by product/age/weight","field":"maximum_approved_daily_dose","proposed_value":"NAYZILAM: do not use more than 2 doses to treat a single seizure-cluster episode; recommended no more than 1 episode every 3 days and no more than 5 episodes per month. SEIZALAM: recommended dose 10 mg IM once for adults with status epilepticus.","rationale":"The NAYZILAM label provides explicit maximum treatment frequency, and SEIZALAM provides a 10 mg IM recommended adult dose.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Rescue/status dosing is intermittent, not chronic daily dosing","field":"typical_doses_per_day","proposed_value":"Rescue/status dosing is intermittent, not chronic daily dosing","rationale":"NAYZILAM and SEIZALAM are labeled for intermittent acute use rather than chronic daily administration.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"1.5-6 h","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"1.8-6.4 h in healthy adult IV studies; 2.1-6.2 h following NAYZILAM in clinical trials","rationale":"Checked FDA labels support 1.8-6.4 h for IV studies and 2.1-6.2 h for NAYZILAM; the row's 1.5 h lower bound was not verified.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215868s000lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Liver and gut","field":"major_organ_for_metabolism","proposed_value":"Liver and gut","rationale":"FDA labels state CYP3A4 metabolism in liver and gastrointestinal tract/gut mucosa.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215868s000lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Gut; Liver/hepatic","field":"filter_metabolism","proposed_value":"Gut; Liver/hepatic","rationale":"Matches FDA labeling for CYP3A4 metabolism in liver and gut/GI tract mucosa.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215868s000lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Not an inducer/inhibitor; CYP3A substrate","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting","proposed_value":"CYP3A4 substrate; exposure is increased by CYP3A4 inhibitors and decreased by CYP3A4 inducers; not classified as an enzyme-inducing antiseizure medication","rationale":"FDA labels support CYP3A4 metabolism and clinically relevant CYP3A inhibitor/inducer effects. The exact negative claim 'not an inducer/inhibitor' is not directly stated in the checked label text, so the proposed wording is more source-concordant.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Inhibitor; Substrate / affected by modulators","field":"filter_enzyme_effect","proposed_value":"Substrate / affected by modulators","rationale":"The row's 'Inhibitor' filter describes CYP3A inhibitors that affect midazolam, not midazolam itself. FDA labeling supports midazolam as a CYP3A4 substrate affected by inhibitors/inducers.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Benzodiazepine positive allosteric modulator of GABA-A receptors; enhances GABAergic inhibition by binding at the benzodiazepine site.","field":"mechanism_of_action","proposed_value":"Benzodiazepine positive allosteric modulator of GABA-A receptors; enhances GABAergic inhibition by binding at the benzodiazepine site.","rationale":"FDA labels describe potentiation of GABAergic neurotransmission through binding at the benzodiazepine site of the GABAA receptor.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"High","field":"mechanism_confidence","proposed_value":"High","rationale":"The mechanism is well established for benzodiazepines and is supported by FDA labels.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"American Epilepsy Society 2024 U.S. ASM summary; FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA NAYZILAM label; FDA SEIZALAM label; FDA midazolam injection label","rationale":"The checked FDA labels directly support mechanism. DailyMed is not in the trusted source list for this audit, and AES was not needed to support the mechanism cell.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215868s000lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"AES/FDA summary","field":"mechanism_source_tier","proposed_value":"FDA label","rationale":"FDA labeling directly supports the mechanism field and is the trusted source tier actually used here.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"GABA","field":"filter_mechanism","proposed_value":"GABA","rationale":"Consistent with benzodiazepine potentiation of GABAA receptor signaling.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"CNS: somnolence/sedation 10%, respiratory: nasal discomfort 16% for nasal product, throat irritation 3%, respiratory depression 2%, GI: nausea 2%","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"Clinical-trial adverse reactions (selected): NAYZILAM comparative phase: somnolence 10%, nasal discomfort 9% overall (16% in 5 mg + 5 mg subgroup), throat irritation 3%, rhinorrhea 3%; SEIZALAM status-epilepticus trial: upper airway obstruction 5%, agitation 4%, pyrexia 4%, mental status changes 3%, postictal state 3%, acute renal failure 2%; IV midazolam single-agent sedation: nausea 2.8%; pediatric IV literature: apnea 2.8%.","rationale":"Nasal discomfort is an application-site adverse reaction, not a respiratory category. The checked FDA labels did not verify 'respiratory depression 2%' or 'nausea 2%' as written; FDA labeling supports different adverse-event terms and percentages.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215868s000lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"CNS; GI; Respiratory","field":"filter_symptom_category","proposed_value":"CNS; GI; Respiratory; Application site","rationale":"NAYZILAM adverse reactions include somnolence/CNS and nasal discomfort/throat irritation/rhinorrhea; the nasal discomfort item is best captured as application site/local as well as not purely respiratory.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215868s000lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"No clinically meaningful QT effect established","field":"qt_interval_effect","proposed_value":"No clinically meaningful QT effect established","rationale":"This is plausible, but the row bundle does not cite a trusted source that directly evaluates QT effect. FDA labels checked for other facts do not establish a clinically meaningful QT signal.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"No known meaningful QT effect","field":"filter_qt_effect","proposed_value":"No known meaningful QT effect","rationale":"Same source limitation as qt_interval_effect; no direct trusted QT-specific source was supplied in the row.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Epilepsy Foundation Australia ASM list; Wikipedia anticonvulsant drug-class list; American Epilepsy Society 2024 U.S. ASM summary; FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA/openFDA and FDA AccessData labels for NAYZILAM, SEIZALAM, and midazolam injection; FDA Orphan Drug Designations and Approvals for SEIZALAM; EMA/eMC SmPC for buccal/oromucosal midazolam; PubMed RCT records; ClinicalTrials.gov records","rationale":"Wikipedia, Epilepsy Foundation Australia, AES, and DailyMed are not in the provided trusted-source domain list for this audit. The proposed sources directly support the row's regulatory, formulation, indication, mechanism, and RCT facts.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%222b29422e-54d5-4a49-8522-e9cf752368c3%22&limit=5","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=508215","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33140403/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31140596/"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed SPL; status=boxed_warning_found; setid=2b29422e-54d5-4a49-8522-e9cf752368c3; published=Jan 31, 2023; title=NAYZILAM (MIDAZOLAM) SPRAY [UCB, INC.]; url=https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=2b29422e-54d5-4a49-8522-e9cf752368c3","field":"fda_black_box_warning_source","proposed_value":"FDA/openFDA drug label API; status=boxed_warning_found; spl_set_id=2b29422e-54d5-4a49-8522-e9cf752368c3; effective_time=20230119; title=NAYZILAM (MIDAZOLAM) nasal spray; api_url=https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%222b29422e-54d5-4a49-8522-e9cf752368c3%22&limit=5; fda_label_pdf=https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","rationale":"DailyMed is explicitly not permissible for the FDA black-box warning field; openFDA and FDA AccessData are permissible FDA sources.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%222b29422e-54d5-4a49-8522-e9cf752368c3%22&limit=5","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Benzodiazepine used for seizure clusters/status epilepticus rescue; also used for procedural sedation/anesthesia.","field":"status_or_notes","proposed_value":"Benzodiazepine used for seizure clusters/status epilepticus rescue; also used for procedural sedation/anesthesia.","rationale":"Supported by FDA labels for NAYZILAM, SEIZALAM, and injectable midazolam sedation/anesthesia use.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/211321s008lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/209566s003lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215868s000lbl.pdf"]}],"generic_name":"midazolam","missing_rcts":[],"outcome_checks":[{"current_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo RR50 differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","field":"diff_50_responder_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo RR50 differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","rationale":"The retained placebo-controlled midazolam nasal spray RCT abstracts report acute seizure-cluster endpoints, not chronic >=50% responder-rate outcomes.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33140403/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31140596/"]},{"current_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo MPC differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","field":"diff_median_pct_change_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo MPC differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","rationale":"The retained acute rescue RCTs do not report a median percent seizure-frequency change endpoint suitable for this field.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33140403/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31140596/"]},{"current_value":"16.1-19.3 % (drug minus placebo seizure-freedom differential at maximum effective dose/regimen: Spencer2020 midazolam nasal spray 5 mg 16.1%; Detyniecki2019 midazolam nasal spray 5 mg 19.3%)","field":"diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"16.1-19.3% drug-minus-placebo acute seizure-control differential at 5 mg intranasal midazolam: Spencer2020 6-hour seizure freedom 54.8% vs 38.7%, difference 16.1%; Detyniecki2019 treatment success/no recurrence through 6 hours 53.7% vs 34.4%, difference 19.3%.","rationale":"The numeric differentials are supported, but Detyniecki2019 reports treatment success, not a pure seizure-freedom endpoint. The wording should distinguish the endpoints.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33140403/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31140596/"]},{"current_value":"Spencer2020|16.1|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33140403/|62; Detyniecki2019|19.3|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31140596/|201","field":"plot_diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"Spencer2020|16.1|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33140403/|62; Detyniecki2019|19.3|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31140596/|201","rationale":"The values and sample sizes match the abstracts/local outcome rows; note the Detyniecki value is treatment success/no recurrence, not strict seizure freedom.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33140403/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31140596/"]},{"current_value":"","field":"plot_diff_50_responder_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"","rationale":"Blank is appropriate because no RR50 differential was extractable from the retained acute rescue RCTs.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"not_applicable","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33140403/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31140596/"]},{"current_value":"","field":"plot_diff_median_pct_change_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"","rationale":"Blank is appropriate because no median percent change differential was extractable from the retained acute rescue RCTs.","requires_user_approval":false,"s